## [ENT-210] | 2026-11-25 12:08 UTC | Zero | error | ref: game/fleet/mine_net.py | see: queue.log ENT-335
What: mine-network's belt score ranked far station legs above near ones for every training-ore belt. A training belt's score is below zero (its fuel penalty exceeds what its full good is worth), and the score was (value - fuel) / (1 + 0.6 x hops + 1.2 x station hops): dividing a negative number by a longer way raises it. Claim lines of 2026-11-25 read 4 jumps to the belt and 4 more to the station; with most of the division on training ore since iron went full (pull record, 02:10 UTC), every load paid that leg.
Cause: a score that subtracts a cost and then divides by distance is monotone in distance only while it stays positive; the tiers above zero (deficit, craft demand, market) hid it until nothing was short.
Fix: b1269f9a (fleet-build, 122 test files green): a negative score is multiplied by the way, a positive one divided as before. Test MineNetExperienceScoreTests (five systems in a line), red before. On the live store the best belts' way to a station falls from 2.55 to 2.35 jumps on average. Would it have caught the original: yes, the test is that case.
Class: any score of the form (gain - cost) / (1 + distance) inverts when gain < cost. @Apophis: game/fleet/hunt_net.py line 916 has that form, ((loot_est x fit_mult) - fuel_est) / (1 + 0.7 x hops + 0.8 x st_hops): a herd whose loot is below its fuel ranks higher the farther it lies.
